# macOS Qt session parity

## Authorized scope

Develop on `macos-qt-session`, starting from root main
`21a253aecd4ea75a88d7be6d4b375b955c8d64bd` and Client main
`2ed1c5f66e90799659002faed6d8f0961c0d4c6f`. Bring the macOS streaming window
and controls to the shared Qt frontend without changing the Host or protocol.
Do not integrate the separate, unfinished Apple-native frontend.

Retain one Apple Silicon Client package for macOS15 and newer, compiled with
SDK27 or newer and the existing pinned dependencies. Hosted Actions perform
builds and automated checks; installed session acceptance is a separate gate.
Do not publish or merge an incomplete port. Ubuntu's accepted Qt/PipeWire path
and the current macOS SDL/Metal frontend remain the comparison baselines.

## Implementation order

1. Prove native Metal rendering and Qt controls share an exact 10-bit SDR
   target. Qt6.10.2's ordinary Metal SDR swapchain selects BGRA8, regardless
   of the requested RGB channel sizes. Do not accept that reduction or label
   SDR identity video as HDR to obtain a wider framebuffer. Investigate the
   public QQuickRenderControl/QQuickRenderTarget path with an explicit
   RGB10A2 Metal texture and the existing production video shader. Verify all
   1,024 gray levels and independent RGB ramps after Qt composition, including
   5K, resize and target replacement. An offscreen GPU test does not qualify
   WindowServer, the physical panel or actual decoder support.
2. Separate the shared Qt scene, toolbar, presentation geometry, bounded frame
   mailbox and recovery controller from Linux EGL/VA-API/Wayland components.
   Add a macOS window/Metal adapter using public Qt rendering integration.
   Reuse the existing VideoToolbox/CoreVideo texture import and software-plane
   formats, color parameters and scaling math. Never read hardware-decoded
   frames back to the CPU or silently substitute another codec/profile.
3. Wire macOS input and lifetime adapters: keyboard scan codes, Accessibility
   capture with Spaces/focus recovery, ordinary Quit versus remote Command-Q,
   normalized tablet input and existing raw-Wacom ownership. Reuse clipboard,
   microphone, camera and permission policy. Preserve custom cursors and keep
   toolbar input local. Retain the existing macOS SDL/CoreAudio playback
   backend initially; direct PipeWire is Linux-only, not a generic Qt choice.
4. Match Ubuntu's session controls: toolbar layout/icons/telemetry, reveal and
   pinning, display selection, aspect-preserving presentation, window restore
   size, and automatic network recovery with a Disconnect-only status dialog.
   Preserve native Mac fullscreen Spaces, Retina logical/backing coordinates,
   notch-safe content, multiple displays and three-finger switching. Do not
   copy Linux compositor workarounds into Cocoa without evidence.
5. Build branch-qualified candidates, then qualify the exact signed package
   on supported macOS versions. Keep the same installed Client identity,
   bookmarks, Host trust, administrator policy and logs. Update documented
   behavior only after the corresponding path is implemented and verified.

## Gates

- Exact-format hardware decode qualification and exact software fallback;
  8/10-bit, 4:4:4 identity and existing YCbCr profiles, 4K and 5120x2160.
- One bounded rendering pipeline; no unbounded frame queue, GUI-thread
  network/decoder waits, or resource release before GPU completion. Confirm
  presentation feedback remains the audio/video synchronization clock.
- Shared Ubuntu regression checks after common changes. On Mac, test Metal
  precision and cleanup, toolbar event delivery, fullscreen/focus transitions,
  repeated connection/recovery/cancel/Quit and media/input permission timing.
- Real Mac sessions to Linux and macOS Hosts: smooth video, A/V soak, input,
  Wacom pressure/buttons, clipboard, microphone, camera, resize and display
  changes. Compilation or fixture success is not installed acceptance.
- Keep the operator's active Terminal and other sessions running. No unattended
  logout, reboot or installation on an occupied machine.

## Source references

- Qt6.10.2 `qtbase/src/gui/rhi/qrhimetal.mm`, `chooseFormats()`.
- Qt6.10.2 `qtdeclarative/src/quick/scenegraph/qsgrhisupport_mac.mm`.
- [QQuickRenderControl](https://doc.qt.io/qt-6.10/qquickrendercontrol.html).
- Existing Client `vt_metal.mm`, `vt_colors.h`, `vt_renderer.metal` and
  `streaming/qt/` implementation and tests.

## Current state

The initial real GPU composition gate passed on GitHub run 41262235706,
including all ten-bit levels at 4K/5K. The branch now selects a Qt-controlled
Mac window backed by a native RGB10A2 SDR drawable. A dedicated worker acquires
drawables and completes GPU submissions; only scene synchronization briefly
holds the GUI. Shared frame feedback, exact-format decoder qualification and
a single-slot delivery mailbox are reused. Production CVPixelBuffer import,
software-plane rendering and Mac UI/lifecycle tests are being qualified.

Mac input reuses the existing Accessibility tap and raw-HID owner with a Qt
queue wake, preserving its bounded queue and focus recovery. Qt Cocoa physical
key mapping and normalized pen fallback are separate from Linux input. Native
Spaces, Retina/notch geometry, clipboard and application Quit retain their
existing policy. Audio playback remains SDL/CoreAudio. No dependency, Host or
protocol changes, installed acceptance, merge or release have occurred.
See HANDOFF for exact tested commits and the remaining gates.
